course: dictionary
lesson: 15085
title: Risen: going up
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Risen» means has gone up or got higher. It is the form of «rise» that you use after has, have or had. «The sun has risen over the hill.» The sun is going up in the sky, so risen means it has come up.
explain: The clues are often in the words around it. «The temperature has risen by 5 degrees» tells you the number got bigger, so the temperature went up. «Prices have risen again this year» means the prices went up, so people pay more. Ask yourself: has it gone up, or has it gone down?
explain: Watch the three forms: rise, rose, risen. Use rose on its own: «Yesterday the sun rose at six.» Use risen after has, have or had: «Today the sun has risen at five.» Rise is the everyday word for going up, as in «The river rises after the rain».
fact: Risen means having gone up or got higher.
fact: Risen is used for the sun or moon coming up, such as «The moon has risen».
fact: Risen is used for prices, numbers or water going up, such as «The water has risen».
fact: Risen can also mean got up from bed or from a chair, as in «Sam had risen from his chair».
fact: Rise, rose, risen: use rose on its own, and risen after has, have or had.
exercise: «The sun has risen over the hill.» What does risen mean here? | Gone down | Hidden behind clouds | *Come up, higher in the sky | Fallen to the ground
exercise: «The temperature has risen by 5 degrees.» Has the temperature gone up or down? | Gone down | Stayed the same | *Gone up
exercise: «Prices have risen again this year.» Copy the word that tells you the prices went up. | this | prices | again | *risen
test: «The river has risen after the heavy rain.» What does has risen mean? | *Gone up | Frozen solid | Gone down | Dried up
test: Fill the gap: «Yesterday the sun ___ at six. Today it has ___ at five.» | rose | *risen | rise | rising
exam: Which word fits? «Sam had ___ from his chair when the bell rang.» | rising | rose | rise | *risen
